Subject: How run works in Jps script — Simple 2D Game LogicHi everyone,I am building a custom, highly simplified 2D programming language called Jps script. The core focus of Jps script is to let anyone create 2D games with just a few lines of code without writing complex boilerplate game loops.Here is how the dynamic movement system (run) works in Jps script:1. Default State (Static Object)If you define an object without calling the run method, the object remains static (it stays fixed at its position).Plaintextimport.box[cite: 1]

a = body_2D.box()[cite: 1]
b = size.box(500,500)[cite: 1]
# Note: run.box() is NOT called here[cite: 1]
Behavior: The 2D Box will spawn on the screen, but it will remain stationary (ruka rahega).2. Active State (Automatic Movement)When you include run.box(), Jps script automatically attaches the background physics and render loop to make the object move automatically (chalne lagega).Plaintextimport.box[cite: 1]

a = body_2D.box()[cite: 1]
b = size.box(500,500)[cite: 1]
c = run.box()[cite: 1]
Behavior: The moment run.box() is executed, the box starts moving dynamically across the scene.Why this design?In traditional game engines, moving a box requires handling event loops, delta time, $X/Y$ velocity updates, and screen redrawing. In Jps script, 1 single function line (run.box()) handles all underlying execution loops behind the scenes!





